C.3 Proof of Proposition B.2 : From affine estimates to the half-turn bound The following proposition controls the part of the \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}} -spectrum near +1 . Proposition C.5 (Interpolation bound on the \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}} -spectral subspace associated with [\frac{1}{2},1] ) . Assume \beta\eta\leq c . If w\in\operatorname{ran}(\mathbf{1}_{[1/2,1]}(\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})) , then \|(I+\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp})\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}w\|^{2}\leq C\,\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}w\|\,\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{3}w\|\,. (C.14) We now prove Proposition B.2 . Proof of Proposition B.2 . We regard \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}} and \mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}} as operators on \mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P},0} and work with the spectral subspaces \mathcal{H}\coloneqq\operatorname{ran}(\mathbf{1}_{[1/2,1]}(\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}))\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\mathcal{H}^{\prime}\coloneqq\operatorname{ran}(\mathbf{1}_{[-1/2,1/2)}(\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}))\,. Recall that \mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}=\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-1} , and therefore Proposition C.5 yields \displaystyle\|(I+\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp})\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}v\|^{2} \displaystyle=\|(I+\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp})\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{-1}v\|^{2}\leq C\,\|v\|\,\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{2}v\|\,\hskip 20.00003pt\forall v\in\mathcal{H}\,. Recall that by Lemma B.1 , \gamma_{\rm gap}^{2}I\preceq\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{2}\preceq I . Applying Lemma D.4 with \mathsf{A}=C^{-1/2}\,(\mathsf{T}^{*}\mathsf{T})^{1/2} , \mathsf{B}=\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{2}|_{\mathcal{H}} , m=\gamma_{\rm gap}^{2} , and M=1 , where \mathsf{T}\coloneqq(I+\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp})\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}|_{\mathcal{H}} , gives a factor 1+\log(1/\gamma_{\rm gap}) , up to a universal constant. Shrinking the universal constant c in the assumption if necessary, we may suppose that \beta\eta\leq 1/16 . Then \gamma_{\rm gap}\leq 2\sqrt{\alpha\eta}\leq 1/2 , so the additive constant is absorbed by \log(1/\gamma_{\rm gap}) . Therefore, \displaystyle\|(I+\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp})\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}v\|^{2}\leq C\log(1/\gamma_{\rm gap})\,\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}v\|^{2}\,\hskip 20.00003pt\forall v\in\mathcal{H}\,. On the other hand, on \mathcal{H}^{\prime} , no further spectral localization is needed. Since \mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp} is a contraction, \mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}} is an isometry, and \mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{2}\succeq\frac{3}{4}I on \mathcal{H}^{\prime} , we have \|(I+\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp})\mathsf{V}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}v\|\leq 2\,\|v\|\leq 4\,\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}v\|\,\hskip 20.00003pt\forall v\in\mathcal{H}^{\prime}\,. For a general v\in\mathfrak{H}_{\mathsf{P},0} , decompose v=v_{\mathcal{H}}+v_{\mathcal{H}^{\prime}} . Combining the preceding two estimates with (a+b)^{2}\leq 2a^{2}+2b^{2} , and using that \mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}} commutes with \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}} , proves ( B.17 ). ∎ We prove Proposition C.5 after the following preparatory results. The first approximates \mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}w , conditionally on Y_{+} , by a function that is affine in the rescaled coordinate W=(X-Y_{+})/\sqrt{\eta} , so that Proposition C.2 applies. The second bounds the resulting affine data by the gradients of w and (I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})w , using the derivative formula ( C.8 ). Both results hold for arbitrary functions in H^{1}(\pi_{\eta}^{Y}) ; the spectral condition enters when we invert \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}} . Recall from Section C.2 the conditional law q_{y}\coloneqq\Law(W\mid Y_{+}=y) , its mean \bar{w}_{y} and covariance \Sigma_{y} , and that, conditionally on Y_{+}=y , we have X=y+\sqrt{\eta}\,W and Y_{-}=y+2\sqrt{\eta}\,W . Lemma C.6 (Conditional affine approximation and energy bound) . Let w\in H^{1}(\pi_{\eta}^{Y}) , and let a_{y}\coloneqq\mathbb{E}[\nabla w(Y_{-})\mid Y_{+}=y] denote the conditional mean of the gradient. For \pi_{\eta}^{Y} -almost every y , define e_{y}(u)\coloneqq w(y+2\sqrt{\eta}u)-(\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}w)(y)-2\sqrt{\eta}\,a_{y}^{\top}(u-\bar{w}_{y})\,,\hskip 20.00003ptu\in\mathbb{R}^{d}\,. Then, e_{y} has q_{y} -mean zero, the decomposition \mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}w=2\sqrt{\eta}\,a_{Y_{+}}^{\top}(W-\bar{w}_{Y_{+}})+e_{Y_{+}}(W) (C.15) holds in L^{2}(\pi_{\eta}) , and, with \mathcal{R}\coloneqq\|\nabla w\|^{2}-\int\|a_{y}\|^{2}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)=\mathbb{E}\|\nabla w(Y_{-})-a_{Y_{+}}\|^{2}\,, (C.16) one has \mathbb{E}[e_{Y_{+}}(W)^{2}]\leq 4\eta\mathcal{R}\,. (C.17) Moreover, if \beta\eta\leq c for a sufficiently small universal constant c>0 , then \mathcal{R}+\int a_{y}^{\top}(I-\Sigma_{y})a_{y}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)\leq C\,\|\nabla(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})w\|\,\|\nabla w\|\,. (C.18) Proof. By definition, \mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}w=w(Y_{-})-\mathbb{E}[w(Y_{-})\mid Y_{+}]=w(Y_{-})-(\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}w)(Y_{+})\,. Conditioning on Y_{+}=y and substituting Y_{-}=y+2\sqrt{\eta}\,W gives, for \pi_{\eta}^{Y} -almost every y , (\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}w)(u)=w(y+2\sqrt{\eta}u)-(\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}w)(y)\,,\hskip 20.00003ptu\in\mathbb{R}^{d}\,, which is ( C.15 ). This function belongs to L^{2}(q_{y}) and has q_{y} -mean zero; since the subtracted affine function is q_{y} -centered, e_{y} has q_{y} -mean zero as well. The coefficient 2\sqrt{\eta}\,a_{y} is chosen to center the gradient of e_{y} : differentiating the preceding identity and averaging under q_{y} gives \int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}\nabla_{u}(\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}w)(u)\,q_{y}(\mathrm{d}u)=2\sqrt{\eta}\,\mathbb{E}[\nabla w(Y_{-})\mid Y_{+}=y]=2\sqrt{\eta}\,a_{y}\,, and hence \nabla_{u}e_{y}(W)=2\sqrt{\eta}\,\{\nabla w(Y_{-})-a_{y}\}\,. Since q_{y} has Poincaré constant at most 1 , it follows that \mathbb{E}[e_{y}(W)^{2}\mid Y_{+}=y]\leq\mathbb{E}[\|\nabla_{u}e_{y}(W)\|^{2}\mid Y_{+}=y]=4\eta\,\mathbb{E}[\|\nabla w(Y_{-})-a_{y}\|^{2}\mid Y_{+}=y]\,. Integrating in y and expanding the square using the definition of a_{y} proves ( C.17 ). We turn to the energy estimate. We may assume \beta\eta\leq 1/2 . Recall that g_{y}(u)=\sqrt{\eta}\,\nabla V(y+\sqrt{\eta}u) , so that \nabla V(X)=\eta^{-1/2}g_{y}(W) , and that \mathrm{Cov}(W,g_{y}(W)\mid Y_{+}=y)=I-\Sigma_{y} by Lemma C.4 . The decomposition ( C.15 ) therefore gives \mathrm{Cov}(w(Y_{-}),\nabla V(X)\mid Y_{+}=y)=2(I-\Sigma_{y})a_{y}+\eta^{-1/2}\varepsilon_{y}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\varepsilon_{y}\coloneqq\mathrm{Cov}(e_{y}(W),g_{y}(W)\mid Y_{+}=y)\,. Substituting this into ( C.8 ) yields [\nabla(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})w](y)=\nabla w(y)-a_{y}+2(I-\Sigma_{y})a_{y}+\eta^{-1/2}\varepsilon_{y}\,. (C.19) Conditional Cauchy–Schwarz and Lemma C.4 give, for every v\in\mathbb{R}^{d} , |\varepsilon_{y}^{\top}v|^{2}\leq C\beta\eta\,\mathbb{E}[e_{y}(W)^{2}\mid Y_{+}=y]\,v^{\top}(I-\Sigma_{y})v\,. (C.20) We will also use 0\preceq I-\Sigma_{y}\preceq\beta\eta I\,. (C.21) The two-sided bound in Lemma B.1 , together with the fact that \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}} fixes constants, gives (I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})^{2}\preceq 3\,(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2}) on L^{2}(\pi_{\eta}^{Y}) . Since a_{y}=(\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}\nabla w)(y) , with \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}} acting coordinatewise, \mathcal{R}=\bigl\langle\nabla w,(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{2})\nabla w\bigr\rangle\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\int\|\nabla w(y)-a_{y}\|^{2}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)\leq 3\mathcal{R}\,. (C.22) We now control \mathcal{R} and the affine energy simultaneously. Take the L^{2}(\pi_{\eta}^{Y}) inner product of ( C.19 ) with the vector field y\mapsto\nabla w(y)+a_{y} . Since \int\{\nabla w(y)-a_{y}\}^{\top}\{\nabla w(y)+a_{y}\}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)=\mathcal{R}\,, we obtain the exact identity \displaystyle\mathcal{R}+4\int a_{y}^{\top}(I-\Sigma_{y})a_{y}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y) \displaystyle=\int[\nabla(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})w](y)^{\top}\{\nabla w(y)+a_{y}\}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y) \displaystyle\hskip 20.00003pt-2\int a_{y}^{\top}(I-\Sigma_{y})\{\nabla w(y)-a_{y}\}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y) \displaystyle\hskip 20.00003pt-\eta^{-1/2}\int\varepsilon_{y}^{\top}\{\nabla w(y)+a_{y}\}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)\,. (C.23) We bound the three terms on the right separately. For the first, conditional Jensen gives \int\|a_{y}\|^{2}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)\leq\|\nabla w\|^{2} , and hence \Bigl\lvert\int[\nabla(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})w](y)^{\top}\{\nabla w(y)+a_{y}\}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)\Bigr\rvert\leq 2\,\|\nabla(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})w\|\,\|\nabla w\|\,. (C.24) For the second, Cauchy–Schwarz, ( C.21 ), and ( C.22 ) give \displaystyle 2\,\Bigl|\int a_{y}^{\top}(I-\Sigma_{y})\{\nabla w(y)-a_{y}\}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)\Bigr|\leq C\sqrt{\beta\eta}\,\mathcal{R}^{1/2}\,\Bigl\{\int a_{y}^{\top}(I-\Sigma_{y})a_{y}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)\Bigr\}^{1/2}\,. (C.25) Finally, using the pointwise identity \nabla w(y)+a_{y}=\{\nabla w(y)-a_{y}\}+2a_{y} , ( C.21 ) and ( C.22 ) imply \displaystyle\int\{\nabla w(y)+a_{y}\}^{\top}(I-\Sigma_{y})\{\nabla w(y)+a_{y}\}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)\leq C\beta\eta\,\mathcal{R}+8\int a_{y}^{\top}(I-\Sigma_{y})a_{y}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)\,. (C.26) Using ( C.20 ), Cauchy–Schwarz in y , ( C.17 ), and ( C.26 ), we obtain \displaystyle\eta^{-1/2}\,\Bigl|\int\varepsilon_{y}^{\top}\{\nabla w(y)+a_{y}\}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)\Bigr| \displaystyle\hskip 20.00003pt\leq C\sqrt{\beta\eta\,\mathcal{R}}\,\Bigl\{\beta\eta\,\mathcal{R}+\int a_{y}^{\top}(I-\Sigma_{y})a_{y}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)\Bigr\}^{1/2} \displaystyle\hskip 20.00003pt\leq C\sqrt{\beta\eta}\,\mathcal{R}^{1/2}\,\Bigl\{\int a_{y}^{\top}(I-\Sigma_{y})a_{y}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)\Bigr\}^{1/2}+C\beta\eta\,\mathcal{R}\,. (C.27) Substituting these three estimates into ( C.23 ) gives \displaystyle\mathcal{R}+4\int a_{y}^{\top}(I-\Sigma_{y})a_{y}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y) \displaystyle\leq 2\,\|\nabla(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})w\|\,\|\nabla w\| \displaystyle\hskip 20.00003pt+C\sqrt{\beta\eta}\,\mathcal{R}^{1/2}\Bigl\{\int a_{y}^{\top}(I-\Sigma_{y})a_{y}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)\Bigr\}^{1/2}+C\beta\eta\,\mathcal{R}\,. For sufficiently small universal \beta\eta , the last two terms are absorbed into the left-hand side. This proves ( C.18 ). ∎ Proof of Proposition C.5 . Choose a small enough universal constant in the hypothesis, if necessary, so that Lemma C.6 applies. On \operatorname{ran}(\mathbf{1}_{[1/2,1]}(\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})) , the operator \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{-1} is bounded. Applying ( B.13 ) first to \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{-1}w and then to \mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{-1}(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})w gives \displaystyle\sqrt{\eta}\,\|\nabla w\| \displaystyle\leq C\,\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{-1}w\|\leq C\,\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}w\|\,, \displaystyle\sqrt{\eta}\,\|\nabla(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})w\| \displaystyle\leq C\,\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}}^{-1}(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})w\|\leq C\,\|\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{P}}^{3}w\|\,. (C.28) Here, the final inequality in each line follows by spectral calculus on [\frac{1}{2},1] . Decompose \mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}w according to ( C.15 ). The affine part has coefficient 2\sqrt{\eta}\,a_{y} , so Proposition C.2 , \|I+\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp}\|\leq 2 applied to the error term, and (r+s)^{2}\leq 2r^{2}+2s^{2} give \displaystyle\|(I+\mathsf{H}_{\perp\perp})\mathsf{U}_{\perp\mathsf{P}}w\|^{2} \displaystyle\leq C\beta\eta^{2}\int a_{y}^{\top}(I-\Sigma_{y})a_{y}\,\pi_{\eta}^{Y}(\mathrm{d}y)+C\,\mathbb{E}[e_{Y_{+}}(W)^{2}] \displaystyle\leq C\eta\,\|\nabla(I-\mathsf{U}_{\mathsf{P}\mathsf{P}})w\|\,\|\nabla w\|\,, where the last line uses \beta\eta\leq 1 and Lemma C.6 . Finally, ( C.28 ) gives ( C.14 ). ∎